WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.435
The cross-provider landscape, in each provider’s own words.

2
00:00:03.715 --> 00:00:08.408
Here is what each provider’s documentation emphasizes at time of writing.

3
00:00:08.688 --> 00:00:11.781
Treat this as the claims to test, not as conclusions.

4
00:00:12.061 --> 00:00:13.384
Anthropic (Claude).

5
00:00:13.664 --> 00:00:27.509
The model guide positions the Opus tier for long-running agentic coding and knowledge work, with a 1M-token context window at standard pricing across current Opus and Sonnet models, and Haiku for speed and price.

6
00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:34.531
The documentation leans heavily on agentic and tool-use features, plus controls such as effort and caching.

7
00:00:34.811 --> 00:00:36.667
OpenAI (GPT).

8
00:00:36.947 --> 00:00:50.877
The models page recommends starting with GPT-6 Astra for complex reasoning and coding, lists cheaper Sol and Terra models and an efficient Luna model, and emphasizes reasoning, coding, tool use, and computer use.

9
00:00:51.157 --> 00:00:56.533
All its current models accept text and images, with context windows listed at about 1.

10
00:00:56.813 --> 00:00:58.477
05M tokens.

11
00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:00.037
Google (Gemini).

12
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:08.723
The models page describes Gemini 4 Carbon as its strongest reasoning model and lists Flash and Flash-Lite models for speed and throughput.

13
00:01:09.003 --> 00:01:18.965
The emphasis is native multimodality: the Gemini 4 models accept text, image, audio, video, and PDF input in a 1M-token window.

14
00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:24.152
Google also offers grounding with Google Search on Gemini 3 and newer models.

15
00:01:24.432 --> 00:01:25.776
xAI (Grok).

16
00:01:26.056 --> 00:01:28.275
The models page recommends Grok 4.

17
00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:34.251
7 for code and chat, with context windows from 500K to 1M tokens.

18
00:01:34.531 --> 00:01:46.072
Its distinctive feature is real-time data: the docs state that Grok has no knowledge of current events beyond training unless you enable the server-side Web Search or X Search tools.

19
00:01:46.352 --> 00:01:54.267
X Search lets Grok search posts, users, and threads on X and answer with citations, billed per item fetched plus tokens.

20
00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:55.848
Open-weight models.

21
00:01:56.128 --> 00:02:00.075
You download the weights and run them yourself or through a host of your choice.

22
00:02:00.355 --> 00:02:08.611
Meta’s Llama 4 models (Scout and Maverick, both multimodal) are distributed on Hugging Face under a license you must accept.

23
00:02:08.891 --> 00:02:11.365
The Qwen team publishes the Qwen3.

24
00:02:11.645 --> 00:02:16.147
8 series plus vision and omni (text, audio, image, video) models.

25
00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:19.733
Mistral’s docs list Mistral Small 4 under Apache 2.

26
00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:23.832
0 and Mistral Large 4 under its own open-weight license.

27
00:02:24.112 --> 00:02:31.365
DeepSeek serves its models through an OpenAI-compatible API, and Mistral’s catalog lists a DeepSeek V4.

28
00:02:31.645 --> 00:02:33.992
1 model under the MIT license.

29
00:02:34.272 --> 00:02:40.523
OpenAI’s gpt-oss models (120B and 20B parameters, Apache 2.

30
00:02:40.803 --> 00:02:47.757
0) are documented to fit a single 80 GB GPU and 16 GB of memory respectively.

31
00:02:48.037 --> 00:02:54.629
The strengths are control: data never leaves your infrastructure, you can fine-tune, and there is no per-token fee.

32
00:02:54.909 --> 00:03:03.123
The costs move to GPUs, operations, and keeping up with releases, and licenses differ in what they allow, so read each one.
